        <li>Use inspection tools effectively: head, tail, info, describe for exploratory data analysis</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section class="section" id="what-is-dataframe">
      <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
      <h2>What is a DataFrame?</h2>
      <p>A DataFrame is a two-dimensional labeled data structure with columns of potentially different types. Think of it as a spreadsheet or SQL table, but with powerful manipulation capabilities built into Python. Unlike NumPy arrays which enforce uniform data types, DataFrames can contain mixed types: integers, floats, strings, dates, categories, and more—all in the same table.</p>

      <p>The DataFrame is the heart of Pandas and modern data science in Python. Whether you're working with CSV files, database queries, API responses, or time-series data, you'll use DataFrames to organize, clean, transform, and analyze your data. The combination of labeled axes (index and columns) with automatic alignment makes data operations intuitive and prevents the subtle bugs that can occur when working with unlabeled arrays.</p>

      <div class="key-concept">
        <strong>Key Concept:</strong> DataFrames align data by labels, not position. When you perform operations on two DataFrames, Pandas automatically aligns them by index and column names. This prevents subtle bugs and makes your code more robust and maintainable.
      </div>

      <h3>DataFrame Anatomy</h3>
      <p>Every DataFrame has key components: index (row labels), columns (column labels), values (the actual data), and dtypes (data types). Understanding these components deeply is essential for working effectively with data and diagnosing issues.</p>

