    <section class="section" id="creating-dataframes">
      <span class="section-label">Core Concepts</span>
      <h2>Creating DataFrames from Various Sources</h2>
      <p>DataFrames are flexible and can be created from many data sources: dictionaries, lists of lists, NumPy arrays, and external files. Understanding the different creation methods helps you work with diverse data formats in real-world scenarios. Each method offers advantages depending on your data structure and use case.</p>

      <h3>From Dictionaries</h3>
      <p>Creating from a dictionary is the most intuitive method. Each key becomes a column name, and each value (a list or array) becomes the column data. This is the most common way to construct test DataFrames.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Method 1: Dictionary of lists (each list becomes a column)</span>
<span class="va">data</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">'Product'</span>: [<span class="st">'Laptop'</span>, <span class="st">'Phone'</span>, <span class="st">'Tablet'</span>],
    <span class="st">'Price'</span>: [<span class="nm">1200</span>, <span class="nm">800</span>, <span class="nm">400</span>],
    <span class="st">'Units'</span>: [<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">12</span>, <span class="nm">8</span>]
}
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">data</span>)

<span class="cm"># Method 2: Dictionary of dictionaries (rows with custom index)</span>
<span class="va">data_rows</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">'Employee1'</span>: {<span class="st">'Dept'</span>: <span class="st">'Sales'</span>, <span class="st">'Salary'</span>: <span class="nm">50000</span>},
    <span class="st">'Employee2'</span>: {<span class="st">'Dept'</span>: <span class="st">'Tech'</span>, <span class="st">'Salary'</span>: <span class="nm">75000</span>}
}
<span class="va">df2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">from_dict</span>(<span class="va">data_rows</span>, <span class="va">orient</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'index'</span>)</code></pre>

      <h3>From Lists and NumPy Arrays</h3>
      <p>Create DataFrames from list of lists or NumPy arrays with explicit column and index names. This is useful when converting data from other formats.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># From list of lists</span>
<span class="va">data_list</span> <span class="op">=</span> [[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>], [<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">6</span>]]
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">data_list</span>, <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'X'</span>, <span class="st">'Y'</span>, <span class="st">'Z'</span>])

<span class="cm"># From NumPy array</span>
<span class="va">arr</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">random</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">4</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>)
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">arr</span>, <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'A'</span>, <span class="st">'B'</span>, <span class="st">'C'</span>])

<span class="cm"># With custom index (row labels)</span>
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(
    <span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">random</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">randn</span>(<span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>),
    <span class="va">index</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Mon'</span>, <span class="st">'Tue'</span>, <span class="st">'Wed'</span>, <span class="st">'Thu'</span>, <span class="st">'Fri'</span>],
    <span class="va">columns</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'Sales'</span>, <span class="st">'Costs'</span>, <span class="st">'Profit'</span>]
)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="selection-operations">
      <span class="section-label">Essential Operations</span>
      <h2>Selection and Indexing Operations</h2>
      <p>Selecting data from DataFrames is fundamental to analysis. Pandas provides multiple ways to access rows, columns, and subsets of data: bracket notation, loc (label-based), and iloc (position-based). Mastering these three methods is essential for working efficiently with data.</p>

      <h3>Column and Row Selection Techniques</h3>
      <p>Column selection with bracket notation is intuitive. Row selection uses loc for label-based access and iloc for position-based access. Understanding the difference prevents errors.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Column selection (returns Series - 1D)</span>
<span class="va">names</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]
<span class="va">names_type</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="va">names</span>)  <span class="cm"># pandas.core.series.Series</span>

<span class="cm"># Multiple column selection (returns DataFrame - 2D)</span>
<span class="va">multiple_cols</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[[<span class="st">'Name'</span>, <span class="st">'Age'</span>]]
<span class="va">df_type</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="fn">type</span>(<span class="va">multiple_cols</span>)  <span class="cm"># pandas.core.frame.DataFrame</span>

<span class="cm"># Row selection by position (iloc - integer location)</span>
<span class="va">first_row</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]  <span class="cm"># Returns Series</span>
<span class="va">rows_0_2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>:<span class="nm">2</span>]  <span class="cm"># Returns DataFrame (rows 0-1)</span>

<span class="cm"># Row selection by label (loc - label-based)</span>
<span class="va">row_by_label</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>]  <span class="cm"># If index is 0, 1, 2 or custom labels</span>

<span class="cm"># Element selection - both return scalars</span>
<span class="va">value1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">loc</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>, <span class="st">'Name'</span>]  <span class="cm"># 'Alice' - by label</span>
<span class="va">value2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">iloc</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>]  <span class="cm"># 75000 - by position</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Boolean Indexing for Filtering</h3>
      <p>Filter rows based on conditions. This is essential for exploratory analysis, data validation, and data cleaning. Boolean indexing is one of the most powerful features in Pandas.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Create boolean mask (Series of True/False)</span>
<span class="va">mask</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Age'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">30</span>
<span class="va">over_30</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">mask</span>]  <span class="cm"># Returns only rows where mask is True</span>

<span class="cm"># Compound conditions (must use &amp; | ~ for and, or, not)</span>
<span class="va">high_earners</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Age'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">30</span>) <span class="op">&amp;</span>
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">75000</span>)
]

<span class="cm"># Using isin for membership testing</span>
<span class="va">names_of_interest</span> <span class="op">=</span> [<span class="st">'Alice'</span>, <span class="st">'Carol'</span>]
<span class="va">selected</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">isin</span>(<span class="va">names_of_interest</span>)]

<span class="cm"># Using string methods with boolean indexing</span>
<span class="va">starts_with_a</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">startswith</span>(<span class="st">'A'</span>)]</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="arithmetic-operations">
      <span class="section-label">Data Transformation</span>
      <h2>Arithmetic Operations and Column Manipulation</h2>
      <p>DataFrames support vectorized arithmetic operations across entire columns. These operations are fast because they execute in compiled C code, not in Python loops. This is where the power of Pandas becomes apparent.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Create new columns from arithmetic operations</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Total_Revenue'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Units'</span>]

<span class="cm"># Operations with scalar values (broadcast to all rows)</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price_Markup'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">1.1</span>  <span class="cm"># 10% increase</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price_Range'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>] <span class="op">-</span> <span class="nm">100</span>  <span class="cm"># Subtract fixed amount</span>

<span class="cm"># Logical operations create boolean columns</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Premium'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">1000</span>

<span class="cm"># Conditional assignment using np.where</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Category'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">where</span>(
    <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">1000</span>,
    <span class="st">'Expensive'</span>,
    <span class="st">'Affordable'</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Math operations on numeric columns</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price_Log'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">log</span>(<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Price'</span>])</code></pre>

      <h3>String Methods on Text Columns</h3>
      <p>Pandas provides string methods through the .str accessor. These work on columns containing text and support common string operations without explicit loops.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># String case operations</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name_Lower'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">lower</span>()
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name_Upper'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">upper</span>()

<span class="cm"># String length and extraction</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name_Length'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">len</span>()
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'First_Letter'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]  <span class="cm"># First character</span>

<span class="cm"># String replacement and cleanup</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name_Clean'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">strip</span>()  <span class="cm"># Remove whitespace</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name_Replaced'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Name'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">str</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">replace</span>(<span class="st">'a'</span>, <span class="st">'X'</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="apply-functions">
      <span class="section-label">Advanced Techniques</span>
      <h2>Applying Functions to Data</h2>
      <p>Beyond built-in operations, you can apply custom functions to transform data. Pandas provides several mechanisms: map (Series-level), apply (column or row-level), and transform (preserving index). These are essential for complex transformations.</p>

      <h3>Using map for Custom Transformations</h3>
      <p>The map method applies a function or dictionary mapping to each element of a Series. It's useful for categorical mappings and element-wise transformations.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Map with a lambda function</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Age_Group'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Age'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">map</span>(
    <span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="st">'Young'</span> <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">x</span> <span class="op">&lt;</span> <span class="nm">30</span> <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="st">'Senior'</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Map with dictionary (category mapping)</span>
<span class="va">status_map</span> <span class="op">=</span> {<span class="nm">1</span>: <span class="st">'Active'</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>: <span class="st">'Inactive'</span>, <span class="nm">2</span>: <span class="st">'Pending'</span>}
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Status'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'StatusCode'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">map</span>(<span class="va">status_map</span>)</code></pre>

      <h3>Using apply for Row and Column Operations</h3>
      <p>The apply method is more flexible, applying a function across rows (axis=1) or columns (axis=0). This allows complex multi-column transformations.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Apply to columns (axis 0)</span>
<span class="va">column_stats</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[[<span class="st">'Age'</span>, <span class="st">'Salary'</span>]]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">apply</span>(<span class="va">np</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">mean</span>)

<span class="cm"># Apply to rows (axis 1) - operates on each row as Series</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Summary'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">apply</span>(
    <span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">row</span>: <span class="va">f</span><span class="st">"{row['Name']} is {row['Age']} years old"</span>,
    <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>
)

<span class="cm"># Using custom function</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">categorize_salary</span>(<span class="va">salary</span>):
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">salary</span> <span class="op">&lt;</span> <span class="nm">70000</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">'Low'</span>
    <span class="kw">elif</span> <span class="va">salary</span> <span class="op">&lt;</span> <span class="nm">80000</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">'Medium'</span>
    <span class="kw">else</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="st">'High'</span>

<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary_Level'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">apply</span>(<span class="fn">categorize_salary</span>)</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="dataframe-info">
      <span class="section-label">Inspection and Exploration</span>
      <h2>Inspecting and Understanding DataFrames</h2>
      <p>Before analyzing data, you need to understand its structure, types, and contents. Pandas provides several methods to quickly summarize and inspect DataFrames. These should be your first tools when encountering new data.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Display and preview data</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">head</span>())           <span class="cm"># First 5 rows (default)</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">head</span>(<span class="nm">10</span>))        <span class="cm"># First 10 rows</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">tail</span>())           <span class="cm"># Last 5 rows</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">tail</span>(<span class="nm">3</span>))         <span class="cm"># Last 3 rows</span>

<span class="cm"># Full structural information</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">info</span>())           <span class="cm"># Column types, null counts, memory usage</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">describe</span>())       <span class="cm"># Statistics: count, mean, std, min, max, quartiles</span>

<span class="cm"># Get specific attributes</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">shape</span>)           <span class="cm"># (rows, columns) tuple</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">columns</span>)         <span class="cm"># Column names (Index object)</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">index</span>)           <span class="cm"># Row index (Index object)</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">dtypes</span>)          <span class="cm"># Data types of columns</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">memory_usage</span>())  <span class="cm"># Memory usage per column</span></code></pre>

      <div class="callout-box tip">
        <strong>Exploration Best Practice:</strong> Always start with df.info() and df.describe(). These two methods give you 90% of what you need to know about your data before deeper analysis. info() shows data types and missing values; describe() shows statistical distributions. This routine saves hours of debugging later.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="exercises">
      <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
      <h2>Exercises and Challenges</h2>

      <h3>Exercise 1: Create and Explore a DataFrame</h3>
      <p>Create a DataFrame from employee data, add calculated columns, and inspect the result with different exploration methods.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Create employee data</span>
<span class="va">employees</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">'Name'</span>: [<span class="st">'John'</span>, <span class="st">'Jane'</span>, <span class="st">'Jack'</span>, <span class="st">'Jill'</span>],
    <span class="st">'Department'</span>: [<span class="st">'Sales'</span>, <span class="st">'Tech'</span>, <span class="st">'Sales'</span>, <span class="st">'HR'</span>],
    <span class="st">'Salary'</span>: [<span class="nm">50000</span>, <span class="nm">75000</span>, <span class="nm">55000</span>, <span class="nm">60000</span>],
    <span class="st">'Years'</span>: [<span class="nm">2</span>, <span class="nm">5</span>, <span class="nm">3</span>, <span class="nm">4</span>]
}
<span class="va">df</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">pd</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">DataFrame</span>(<span class="va">employees</span>)

<span class="cm"># Add calculated columns</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Annual_Increase'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.05</span>
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Tenure_Salary_Ratio'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">/</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Years'</span>]

<span class="cm"># Inspect with multiple methods</span>
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">head</span>())
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">info</span>())
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">describe</span>())

<span class="cm"># Filter for Tech department</span>
<span class="va">tech_employees</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Tech'</span>]
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">tech_employees</span>)</code></pre>

      <h3>Exercise 2: Column Selection and Boolean Indexing</h3>
      <p>Practice selecting specific data and filtering rows based on multiple conditions. This is fundamental to exploratory data analysis.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Select specific columns</span>
<span class="va">names_and_salaries</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[[<span class="st">'Name'</span>, <span class="st">'Salary'</span>]]

<span class="cm"># Filter high earners (above median)</span>
<span class="va">median_salary</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">median</span>()
<span class="va">high_earners</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="va">median_salary</span>]

<span class="cm"># Complex filter: Tech employees with high salary and experience</span>
<span class="va">senior_tech</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Department'</span>] <span class="op">==</span> <span class="st">'Tech'</span>) <span class="op">&amp;</span>
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">60000</span>) <span class="op">&amp;</span>
    (<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Years'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;=</span> <span class="nm">3</span>)
]
<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">senior_tech</span>)</code></pre>

      <h3>Exercise 3: Data Transformation with apply</h3>
      <p>Use apply to create new columns based on complex logic involving multiple columns and conditional rules.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Create bonus column based on multiple criteria</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">calculate_bonus</span>(<span class="va">row</span>):
    <span class="cm"># Executives and long-term employees get higher bonuses</span>
    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">70000</span> <span class="kw">and</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Years'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">3</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.15</span>
    <span class="kw">elif</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Years'</span>] <span class="op">&gt;</span> <span class="nm">3</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.10</span>
    <span class="kw">else</span>:
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="va">row</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.05</span>

<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Bonus'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">apply</span>(<span class="fn">calculate_bonus</span>, <span class="va">axis</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">1</span>)
<span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Total_Comp'</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Salary'</span>] <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">df</span>[<span class="st">'Bonus'</span>]

<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">df</span>[[<span class="st">'Name'</span>, <span class="st">'Salary'</span>, <span class="st">'Bonus'</span>, <span class="st">'Total_Comp'</span>]])</code></pre>
    </section>

    <div class="key-takeaways">
      <h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>DataFrames are 2D labeled data structures with flexible types—the foundation of data science in Python</li>
        <li>Create DataFrames from dictionaries, lists, arrays, or external files with explicit column and index names</li>
        <li>Navigate DataFrames using bracket notation, loc (labels), and iloc (positions) for precise data access</li>
        <li>Boolean indexing filters rows based on conditions—essential for exploratory analysis and data validation</li>
        <li>Vectorized arithmetic operations work on entire columns without explicit loops, providing speed and readability</li>
        <li>Use apply, map, and string methods to apply custom functions and transformations across data</li>
        <li>Always start exploration with head(), info(), and describe() to understand data structure and distributions</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

